---
/**
 * Rule 21 — empathy before selling: describe the problem better than the
 * visitor could describe it themselves, and say nothing about the product.
 * Rule 9 — this is written from the desk it happened at, not from a template.
 */
---

<section class="ps-screen border-t border-line bg-sunk">
  <!-- Full shell so the left edge lines up with the header; the reading
       measure is capped per element instead of by re-centring the shell. -->
  <div class="ps-shell">
    <p class="ps-kicker">The problem</p>

    <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-[24ch] text-[clamp(1.9rem,4.2vw,2.9rem)]">
      Nobody decides to sit badly.
    </h2>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-[62ch] space-y-6 text-[1.08rem] text-muted">
      <p>
        At nine your back is against the chair. At eleven you lean in to read
        something small and forget to lean out. By three your chin is past your
        collarbone, your shoulders have rolled forward, and the only thing that
        will mention it is the ache at six.
      </p>
      <p>
        The slide is too slow to feel. That is the whole difficulty: it is not a
        decision you can make better, it is a drift you cannot see. Every
        reminder app gets this wrong by buzzing on a timer: at minute thirty it
        buzzes, whether you are folded over the keyboard or sitting like a
        violinist.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink">
        A timer does not know how you are sitting. Something has to look.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
